meta {
  name: 01 Student login
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "{{studentEmail}}",
    "password": "a-student-password",
    "deviceId": "student-device-1",
    "deviceName": "Android"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    bru.setEnvVar("studentAccessToken", res.getBody().accessToken);
    bru.setEnvVar("studentRefreshToken", res.getBody().refreshToken);
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.subscription.studentsMayLearn: eq true
}

docs {
  The password is whatever you chose in `Student Activation / 01 Activate`,
  not one the center set — a center never knows its students' passwords.

  Captures the student tokens for the rest of this folder. Note these are
  SEPARATE from {{accessToken}}, which is the center's. A center token sent to
  a student route is refused outright, and the reverse too; the two verifiers
  do not accept each other's tokens.

  ## What to look at in the response

  `subscription` is the Phase 5 addition:

  ```json
  "subscription": {
    "status": "TRIAL",
    "studentsMayLearn": true,
    "graceEndsAt": null
  }
  ```

  It exists so a client knows where it stands before its first learning call,
  instead of discovering a block through a 403 it cannot explain.

  `status` has one value the policy itself never produces: **NONE**, meaning no
  center governs this student. It is deliberately distinct from BLOCKED — one
  is "you have no school", the other is "your school stopped paying", and only
  the second is worth offering the student a way to fix.

  ## Worth trying

  - Log in as a student with no center (any account from before centers
    existed) -> `status: "NONE"`, `studentsMayLearn: true`.
  - Block the center per `00 How to block and restore`, then log in again ->
    the login still SUCCEEDS, reporting BLOCKED. Refusing the sign-in would
    strand an account whose center may yet pay; the learning routes are what
    refuse them.
  - `subscription` missing entirely is not an error. If the subscription could
    not be read the field is simply absent, because a working login is worth
    more than a cosmetic field. Enforcement never depends on it.
}
